dex)\n    \n    return list(abstract_states.values())\n\ngrid = [[0,2,5,9,13,15,17,19,23],[1,4,8,12,\"X\",\"X\",\"X\",22,25],[3,7,\"X\",\"X\",59,56,\"X\",\"X\",27],[6,11,\"X\",\"X\",57,53,\"X\",33,29],[10,\"X\",58,54,\"X\",49,41,37,32],[14,\"X\",55,50,42,\"X\",48,\"X\",36],[16,\"X\",\"X\",\"X\",38,43,\"X\",47,40],[18,21,\"X\",30,34,\"X\",44,51,46],[20,24,26,28,31,35,39,45,52]]\nstart = 0\ngoal = 52\n\nprint(group_states(grid, start, goal))\n```\nThis code defines a function `group_states` that takes the grid, starting state index, and goal state index as input. It initializes an empty dictionary to store abstract states for each concrete state.\n\nThe `get_abstract_state` function calculates the abstract state representation for a given concrete state by checking all possible movements in up, down, left, and right directions. If the new position is within the grid boundaries and not blocked by \"X\", it adds the movement direction to the list of possibilities.\n\nThe code then iterates through all states in the grid, calculating their abstract states using `get_abstract_state`. It stores each concrete state index with its corresponding abstract state in the `abstract_states` dictionary. Finally, it returns a list of lists containing equivalent concrete states grouped by their abstract states.\n\nRunning this code will output the grouping of states based on their spatial relation and behavior.",
